<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Frederick, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1625">1625</a>–<a href="/years/1679">1679</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Frederick was Duke of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a> and ruler of the Principality of Calenberg from 1665, with his residence at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>. A convert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>, he ruled a Lutheran land and allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. He built up Hanover as his capital and in 1676 appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottfried-wilhelm-leibniz">Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz</a> as his librarian and adviser. He died without sons and was succeeded by his brother Ernest Augustus.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-frederick-duke-of-brunswick-luneburg.jpg" alt="John Frederick, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg" width="320" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johann Hulsman · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johann_Hulsmann_-_Joh_Friedrich_Herzog_von_Braunschweig-Luneberg-Kalenburg_-_1675.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
